// @emit-error monomorphize: unsupported argument type for `self` in a call to `sorted`
// THE REACH OF `std:array`'S GENERIC SURFACE, pinned where a caller will meet it.
//
// `sorted<T>` — and `reverse<T>`, `indexOf<T>`, and every other export in the module —
// admits scalars (`i32`/`i64`/`f64`/`boolean`), `string`, literal unions, and nested
// lists (`i32[][]`), all measured. It does NOT admit a list whose element is a STRUCT.
// The monomorphizer refuses the instantiation at EMIT time, so `vl check` reports the
// program clean and only `vl build`/`vl run` says no.
//
// This is a PRE-EXISTING, module-wide limitation of the monomorphizer, not something
// `sorted` introduced: `tests/cases/generics/error-genapp-tyaram-*.vl` pin the same
// refusal on hand-written generics, and swapping `sorted` for `reverse` below produces
// the same message with the same shape. It is pinned HERE as well because it decides
// what `std:array` can be used for, and because sorting records by a field is the
// single most obvious thing a caller will reach for first.
//
// THE WORKAROUND IS THE IN-TREE IDIOM, and it costs nothing: sort an INDEX list and
// let the comparator read the keys out of parallel arrays it closes over. That is what
// `array-sorted-stability.vl` does, what `compiler/cli.vl` already does twice, and it
// is available precisely because `less` is a plain `(T, T) => boolean` closure rather
// than a key extractor.
//
// When the monomorphizer grows struct elements, this fixture fails loudly — which is
// the point of pinning it. The fix is to delete this file and add the struct case to
// `array-sorted-stability.vl`, where a `{ key, seq }` record is the natural spelling.
import { sorted } from "std:array"

type Row = { key: i32, seq: i32 }

const rows = [{ key: 2, seq: 0 }, { key: 1, seq: 1 }]
print(rows.sorted((a: Row, b: Row) => a.key < b.key).length)
